.TH KILN 1 "2026-11-02" "kiln" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kiln \- a build system with Tcl-based build descriptions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kiln
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ target .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kiln
is a build system that reads build descriptions written in Tcl
(called Kilnfiles) and executes commands in dependency order.
It uses a DAG of file and command nodes, hybrid staleness detection
(stat fingerprint + content hash + command string hash), and
compiler depfile parsing for automatic header dependencies.
.PP
Build artifacts are placed in
.IR build/<triplet>/ ,
where
.I <triplet>
is the target toolchain identifier (e.g.\&
.IR x86_64-linux-gnu ).
The triplet is detected automatically via
.B cc \-dumpmachine
or set explicitly with
.BR \-\-triplet ,
which may be repeated to build several triplets in one run.
.PP
By default
.B kiln
reads
.I ./Kilnfile
and builds the target named by
.B default
in that file, or every target if no default is declared.
Naming one or more
.I target
arguments builds just those targets and the files they depend on.
A target may be a file produced by a
.BR rule ,
a
.B phony
task, or an
.B alias
that expands to several targets.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file" "\fR,\fP \-\-file " file
Read the build description from
.I file
instead of
.IR ./Kilnfile .
May be specified multiple times; files are evaluated in order.
.TP
.BI \-a " action" "\fR,\fP \-\-action " action
Run a built-in action instead of building targets.
See
.B ACTIONS
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-triplet " triplet"
Set the target triplet explicitly, skipping
.B cc \-dumpmachine
detection.
May be given more than once to build several triplets in a single run;
the build description is evaluated once per triplet with
.B $Triplet
set accordingly.
A failure in one triplet does not stop the others, and
.B kiln
exits nonzero if any triplet failed.
.TP
.BI \-\-variant " name"
Set the active build variant, which the standard-library
.B variant
selector uses to choose flags and a build directory.
Defaults to
.BR release .
.TP
.BI \-\-builddir " path"
Set the build directory prefix for rule outputs.
Rule outputs and depfiles are prefixed with
.I path
automatically, and inputs that name a known build output are resolved
to the prefixed path.
Overrides any
.B builddir
command in the Kilnfile.
.TP
.BI \-\-lib " file.tcl"
Load a Tcl library file before evaluating Kilnfiles.
May be specified multiple times; files are loaded in order.
Libraries define procs that Kilnfiles can call.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-stdlib
Skip loading the built-in standard library
.RI ( kiln.tcl ).
Combine with
.B \-\-lib
to substitute a custom standard library.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print commands that would be executed without running them.
.TP
.BI \-j " N"
Run up to
.I N
commands in parallel.
Defaults to the number of online CPUs.
Independent commands run concurrently; a command waits until every
command that produces one of its inputs has finished.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH ACTIONS
Actions are invoked with
.B \-a
and perform operations other than building targets.
Using a flag rather than positional arguments avoids ambiguity
when a build target shares a name with an action.
.TP
.B clean
Delete all output files declared in the DAG, along with the depfiles
rules generate, and remove any parent directories that become empty.
Also removes the staleness database
.RI ( kiln.cdb )
and the build directory if empty.
.TP
.B status
Report, without building, which targets are out of date and why
(a changed input or command, or a missing output), and list entries in
the staleness database whose rule has been removed from the Kilnfile.
.SH KILNFILE SYNTAX
Build descriptions are Tcl scripts.
kiln extends the Tcl interpreter with the following commands:
.SS Build Commands
.TP
.BI "rule " "outputs " { " body " }
Define a build rule.
.I outputs
is a Tcl list of output file paths.
The
.I body
is evaluated in Tcl context and may call:
.RS
.TP
.BI "inputs " files...
Declare input files.
Sets the variable
.BR $in .
May be called multiple times; inputs accumulate.
.TP
.BI "uses " files...
Declare programs or other files the command depends on but that are not
passed as inputs.
They are tracked for staleness exactly like inputs, so a changed
compiler or assembler forces a rebuild, and when a listed file is itself
a build output it is built before the commands that use it.
They are not added to
.BR $in .
.TP
.BI "command " {template}
Set the shell command to execute.
The template is evaluated after the body completes,
so
.BR $out ", " $in ", and " $depfile
are all available for substitution.
Enclose in braces to defer expansion.
.TP
.BI "depfile " path
Declare a compiler-generated dependency file
(e.g.\& from
.BR "cc \-MMD" ).
Sets the variable
.BR $depfile .
.RE
.TP
.BI "phony " "name " { " body " }
Declare a named target that runs a command but produces no file, such
as a run or test task.
The
.I body
uses the same commands as
.BR rule .
A phony target has no output to check for freshness, so it runs
whenever the build reaches it.
Name it on the command line to run it on demand rather than on every build.
.TP
.BI "default " "target ..."
Set the target or targets built when the command line names none.
.TP
.BI "alias " "name " { " target ... " }
Give a single
.I name
to a list of targets, so one argument can stand for many.
An alias may refer to other aliases;
.B kiln
expands them until it reaches real targets.
.TP
.BI "source " filename
Read and evaluate another Tcl file.
Searches the current directory first, then each directory in the
.B $SourcePath
variable.
.TP
.BI "builddir " path
Set the build directory prefix for rule outputs and depfiles, and set
the
.B $BuildDir
variable.
A
.B \-\-builddir
flag on the command line takes precedence over this command.
.PP
A rule output or depfile may name a subdirectory that does not exist yet.
Before running a command,
.B kiln
creates the parent directory of each of its outputs and its depfile, so a
build can mirror a nested source tree without a separate directory step.
.SS Tcl Extensions
.TP
.BI "glob " "pattern ..."
Return a space-separated list of files matching the given
shell glob patterns.
Elements containing whitespace are brace-quoted.
.TP
.BI "file " "subcommand arg"
Path manipulation.
Subcommands:
.BR dirname ,
.BR tail ,
.BR extension ,
.BR rootname .
.TP
.BI "lindex " "list index"
Return element
.I index
from a Tcl list.
.TP
.BI "llength " list
Return the number of elements in a Tcl list.
.TP
Additional commands
Standard Tcl commands are also provided and behave as in Tcl:
.BR list ,
.BR lappend ,
.BR append ,
.BR incr ,
.BR foreach ,
.BR for ,
.BR catch ,
.BR eval ,
and
.B string
(subcommands
.BR length ,
.BR index ,
.BR range ,
.BR match ,
.BR map ,
.BR first ,
.BR last ,
.BR equal ,
.BR tolower ,
.BR toupper ).
.TP
.BI "exec " "command ..."
Run
.I command
through the shell while the Kilnfile is evaluated and return its
standard output with trailing newlines removed.
A command that fails to launch or exits non-zero raises an error, so
wrap it in
.B catch
to probe optionally.
Intended for small probes such as compiler version or host detection.
.TP
.BI "getenv " "name \fR[\fPdefault\fR]\fP"
Return the value of the environment variable
.IR name ,
or
.I default
(or an empty string) when it is unset.
.SS Predefined Variables
.TP
.B $Triplet
The target triplet string.
.TP
.B $Variant
The active build variant, set by
.BR \-\-variant .
.TP
.B $Jobs
Number of parallel jobs.
.TP
.B $DryRun
.B 1
if
.B \-\-dry\-run
was specified, otherwise
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B $SourcePath
Space-separated list of directories searched by
.BR source .
.TP
.B $BuildDir
The build directory prefix, set once
.B builddir
or
.B \-\-builddir
is used.
.SH STANDARD LIBRARY
A small standard library is embedded in the binary and evaluated before
any Kilnfile unless
.B \-\-no\-stdlib
is given.
It defines helper procedures that reduce rule boilerplate.
The C compiler used by the helpers is the global
.BR CC ,
which defaults to
.B cc
and may be overridden from the environment or by setting it in a
Kilnfile; set it per target to cross-compile.
The global
.B RUN
is the launch wrapper used by
.B run
and
.BR test ;
it is empty by default and is read from the environment the same way.
The globals
.B CFLAGS
and
.B LDFLAGS
hold compile and link flags; they start empty and are populated by the
Kilnfile and the
.B variant
selector.
.TP
.BI "cc_program " "out sources flags"
Compile each C source in the
.I sources
list and link the resulting objects into
.IR out .
.TP
.BI "cc_objects " "sources flags"
Define a compile rule for each C source and return the list of object
paths, for linking in a separate rule.
Each object keeps the source's relative path with the extension changed,
mirroring the source tree into the build directory rather than flattening
names, so same-named sources in different directories do not collide.
.TP
.BI "cc_object " "obj src dep flags"
Define a rule that compiles the C source
.I src
to the object file
.IR obj ,
writing a depfile to
.I dep
so header changes are tracked, and passing
.I flags
to the compiler.
.TP
.BI "cp_rule " "dst src"
Define a rule that copies
.I src
to
.IR dst .
.TP
.BI "generate " "out inputs command"
Define a rule that produces
.I out
by running
.IR command ,
with
.I inputs
as dependencies.
A thin wrapper over
.B rule
for generated sources such as a parser table.
The generator reruns only when its inputs or command change, so there is
no need to force it to run and guard its output's timestamp.
.TP
.BI "define_header " "out macro value"
Generate a guarded C header at
.I out
that defines
.I macro
to
.IR value .
The value is embedded in the generator command, so the header is rewritten
only when the value changes.
Pair with
.B git_version
to bake a version into a build.
.TP
.BI "version_header " "out macro"
Generate a C header at
.I out
that defines
.I macro
to the project version from
.BR "git describe" ,
or
.B unknown
when git is unavailable.
The version is probed at configuration time and embedded in the generator
command, so the header is rewritten exactly when the version changes and
nothing that includes it recompiles while the version holds.
This replaces the
.B FORCE
rule and
.B cmp
guard a Makefile needs for the same effect.
.TP
.BI "git_version " base
Refine an authoritative
.I base
version (such as the contents of a tracked
.B VERSION
file) with git.
Returns
.I base
unchanged for a clean release tagged
.IR v<base> ,
.I base
plus the
.B git describe
suffix (commits since the tag, short sha,
.BR \-dirty )
once the tree moves past the tag,
.IR base \-dev\-g<sha>
when no matching tag exists, and
.I base
alone when git is unavailable.
Matching
.I v<base>
keeps a vendored copy from describing itself against a host project's
tags, and the tracked base keeps a tarball or tagless checkout producing a
sane version.
Combine with
.BR define_header :
.RS
.PP
.nf
define_header build/$Triplet/version.h APP_VERSION \e
    [git_version [exec cat VERSION]]
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "run " "name program opts"
Define a phony target
.I name
that depends on
.IR program ,
a build output, and launches it with
.I opts
appended once it is built.
A relative program with no directory part is launched as
.IR ./program .
The launch is wrapped by the global
.B RUN
(empty by default, so the program runs natively; set it to an emulator
such as
.B qemu\-arm
to run a cross-compiled binary).
A non-zero exit fails the build and prints the target, the command, and
its output.
Pass
.B {}
for
.I opts
when there are none.
.TP
.BI "test " "name program opts"
Run a test program the same way as
.BR run .
A test is a program whose exit status is the verdict, so a non-zero exit
fails the build.
.TP
.BI "variant " "name flags"
Register a build variant.
When
.I name
is the active
.B $Variant
.RB ( \-\-variant ,
default
.BR release ),
append
.I flags
to the globals
.B CFLAGS
and
.B LDFLAGS
and set the build directory to
.IR build/<triplet>/<name> ,
so each variant keeps its own outputs and staleness database.
The flags reach both compilation and linking, so a sanitizer variant
works from one entry.
.B CFLAGS
and
.B LDFLAGS
start empty; set common flags before declaring variants, and pass
.B $CFLAGS
to the
.B cc_*
helpers.
.SH STALENESS DETECTION
kiln uses a two-level check for each input file:
.IP 1. 4
Compare the stat fingerprint (mtime, size, inode, ctime) against
the value stored in
.IR kiln.cdb .
If it matches, the file is unchanged (fast path).
.IP 2. 4
If the stat differs, hash the file contents and compare against the
stored hash.
If the hash matches, the file was touched but not modified
(e.g.\&
.BR touch ).
.PP
The command string for each rule is also hashed.
Changing compiler flags or other command arguments triggers a rebuild
even if no input files changed.
.PP
Compiler-generated depfiles
.RB ( "cc \-MMD" )
are parsed after each compilation to discover implicit header
dependencies.
These are stored in
.I kiln.cdb
and checked for staleness on subsequent builds.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I Kilnfile
Build description file (Tcl script).
.TP
.IR build/<triplet>/
Directory for build artifacts.
.TP
.IR build/<triplet>/kiln.cdb
Persistent staleness database (CDB format).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All targets are up to date or were built successfully.
.TP
.B 1
A build command failed, a cycle was detected, or an error occurred.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the default target from
.IR ./Kilnfile :
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build named targets and their dependencies:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln app test
.fi
.RE
.PP
Clean all outputs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln -a clean
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dry run with explicit triplet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln -n --triplet arm-linux-gnueabihf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build two triplets in one run, each with its own toolchain and tree:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln --triplet arm-linux-gnueabihf --triplet riscv64-linux-gnu
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a custom library and a build description other than
.IR ./Kilnfile :
.PP
.RS
.nf
kiln --no-stdlib --lib my-stdlib.tcl -f project.kiln
.fi
.RE
.PP
Example Kilnfile:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set cflags "-Wall -O2"

rule build/$Triplet/hello.o {
    inputs hello.c
    depfile build/$Triplet/hello.d
    command {cc $cflags -c -o $out $in -MMD -MF $depfile}
}

rule build/$Triplet/hello {
    inputs build/$Triplet/hello.o
    command {cc -o $out $in}
}
.fi
.RE
